==English==
{{wikipedia|lang=en}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰeydʰ-}}
15th century, from {{inh|en|enm}}, from {{der|en|frm|fidélité}}, from {{der|en|la|fidēlitās}}, from {{m|la|fidēlis||faithful}}, from {{m|la|fidēs||faith, loyalty}} (English {{m|en|faith}}), from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰidʰ-}}, [[Appendix:Glossary#zero-grade|zero-grade]] of {{m|ine-pro|*bʰeydʰ-||to command, to persuade, to trust}} (English {{m|en|bide}}). {{doublet|en|fealty}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/fɪˈdɛl.ɪ.ti/|/faɪˈdɛl.ɪ.ti/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-fidelity.wav |Audio (UK)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# [[faithfulness|Faithfulness]] to one's [[duties]].
#: {{ux|en|the '''fidelity''' of the civil servants}}
# [[loyalty|Loyalty]] to one's [[spouse]] or [[partner]], including abstention from [[cheat]]ing or [[extramarital]] affairs.
# [[accuracy|Accuracy]], or exact [[correspondence]] to some given [[quality]] or [[fact]].
# The [[degree]] to which a [[system]] accurately [[reproduce]]s an input.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2003|title=Proceedings of the Twenty-ninth International Conference on Very Large Databases, Berlin, Germany, 9-12 September, 2003|page=58
|passage=By placing them closer to the source, we can reduce the number of messages in the system and this in turn is likely to improve the '''fidelity''' of the system.}}

====Quotations====
* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|title=High-'''Fidelity''' Medical Imaging Displays|publisher=Aldo Badano, Michael J. Flynn, Jerzy Kanicki|isbn=0819451916}}
* {{quote-text|en|year=2008|author=David L. Nelson|author2=Michael M. Cox|title=Absolute Ultimate Guide for Lehninger Principles of Biochemistry|isbn=1429212411|section=page S-305
|passage=The isoleucyl-tRNA synthetase has a proofreading function that ensures the '''fidelity''' of the aminoacylation reaction, but the histidyl-tRNA synthetase lacks such a proofreading function.}}

